      <abstract>
<para>Snapper is a command-line tool in &productname; that enables administrators to create and manage Btrfs file system snapshots. By leveraging copy-on-write capabilities, it captures the file system state with minimal disk space overhead, allowing users to safely undo system modifications, restore files, and perform system rollbacks. This provides robust system recovery options while automated cleanup algorithms prevent the partition from running out of space.</para>
<para><emphasis>Prerequisites</emphasis></para>
<itemizedlist>
<listitem>
<para>System root or sudo privileges[cite: 5]</para>
</listitem>
<listitem>
<para>Snapper installed on the system[cite: 5]</para>
</listitem>
<listitem>
<para>A root partition size of at least 16 GB[cite: 5]</para>
</listitem>
</itemizedlist>

        <note>
          <para>This article is the first installment of the series of articles
            about &snapper;. In the subsequent articles, we cover common use cases such
            as undoing changes, rolling back the system, manually creating and managing
            snapshots, automatic snapshot cleanup, and more. Each article builds upon the
            knowledge gained from the previous ones, providing a progressively
            enhanced understanding of the &snapper; tool.</para><!--Add a link to "For more information"
            (to be created) with links to other articles from this series.-->
        </note>
